  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and humid conditions with heat indices \naround 105F expected through the rest of the week. Heat Advisories \nremain in effect for the entire area excluding eastern Currituck/OBX \nand the MD Beaches. \n\nRecords will be challenged today as this heat wave peaks. The \nbackdoor front draped along the coast will lift back to the north as \na warm front and the strong UL ridge will remain anchored just to \nthe west. Actual temperatures should max out in the upper 90s to \naround 100 this afternoon with heat indices peaking between 105 \nand 108. Unlike yesterday, this includes coastal areas since \nthe sfc flow will return to the SW. Heat Advisories are already \nin effect for inland portions of the area (extended from \nyesterday) and advisories for the coastal zones (excluding OBX \nand MD Beaches) go into effect at 11am. The ridge starts to \nbreak down and a cold front will approach from the north going \ninto Friday, but heat advisories will likely need to be extended\nfor at least the southern half of the FA. Afternoon clouds and \nstorms ahead of the front will likely limit temps across the \nnorth. \"Limit\" of course being a relative term since highs are \nstill expected to reach the low 90s. Meanwhile, southern \ncounties will have another day of near 100F temps and heat \nindices over 105. There will finally be some relief from the \nheat on the north side of the cold front over the weekend. Temps\non Sat are a bit uncertain given the early morning frontal \npassage, but highs will generally be in the 80s with some\nlingering 90s across the far south Sat. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There are daily chances for thunderstorms \nthrough Saturday. Some storms may be strong to severe with damaging \nwinds gusts and large hail.\n\nAnother round of weak shortwaves traveling down the ridge will bring \nanother day of potential thunderstorms. Like the last couple of \ndays, there is low confidence in coverage and high confidence that \nanything that does form could be severe. If storms are able to \ninitiate, it would likely be late afternoon for the far NE, then \nprogressing SE through the late evening hours. Once again, damaging \nwind gusts are the main threat. The Day 1 SPC outlook did trim the \nslight back to just above the NC border with a marginal risk for \nalong and south of the border. \n\nThe most favorable overlap of forcing, shear, and instability is \nexpected Friday. At the surface, the aforementioned front to our \nnorth will finally make appreciable progress and descend into our \nlocal area. Aloft, NW flow will remain in place and progressively \nstrengthen as the associated trough drops southward along the NE/Mid-\nAtlantic coast, increasing the deep-layer shear. This all suggests a \nlegitimate severe wx threat for the majority of the area Friday, \nwith the potential for several fast-moving clusters capable of \nwidespread damaging winds, along with isolated supercells and large \nhail. There is a slight risk for the entire area Friday. \n\nThe risk for storms (and any potential severe weather) \nSaturday is dependent on where the front is situated during peak \nheating. After the front moves through, the risk for severe weather \nbecomes negligible as a much drier airmass filters in across the \nregion through at least early next week.",
